Fixes #200. Follow-up to #197.

## What changed

- stores the Zoho millisecond cursor as text so Azure Table Storage does not infer `Edm.Int32` and fail after inserting cases;
- accepts only actual damage descriptions from Allegro instead of treating every `ISSUE` (including delivery delays) as damage;
- correlates the tracking number extracted from a trusted Zoho message through InPost ShipX first and Apaczka second;
- matches a local shipping draft by exact provider reference or a high-confidence combination of provider-owned recipient data;
- never downloads or analyses damage-report PDF attachments;
- preserves an existing tracking number for multi-parcel orders and refuses ambiguous recipient matches;
- hides the legacy delay false-positive from the damage list and badge;
- adds an active state to the damage bell, distinct icons for Damages and the Error queue, readable evidence cards, and spacing between actions.

## Root cause

The Zoho cursor is a 13-digit millisecond timestamp. Azure Tables inferred a Python integer as `Edm.Int32`, so the scan inserted cases and then failed while saving the cursor. Correlation also stopped at local tracking/limited Apaczka history and did not query ShipX by exact tracking number.

## Operational note

The two existing historical InPost numbers are not present in the currently configured ShipX organisation or in all 81 orders visible to the configured Apaczka application. The implementation intentionally leaves them unlinked instead of guessing. Access to the old/second shipping integration (or a separate manual-link fallback) is still needed for those historical records.

