## Summary

Hardens backup creation, validation, restore, and update trust boundaries without changing the normal MISP Docker deployment model.

## What changed

- stage fixed backup artifacts through no-follow file descriptors before validation or restore;
- verify fixed checksum entries and reject unsafe archive paths, links, types, duplicate members, and elevated mode bits;
- require trusted backup roots and create unpredictable, private backup-set directories;
- validate managed restore targets and matching Git origins before destructive operations;
- stop restore when existing Compose cleanup fails;
- preserve validated host-data ownership while neutralizing ownership and permissions for generated configuration;
- regenerate lifecycle state atomically for the actual restore target and resolved upstream commit;
- reject unsafe repository/ref inputs and fail closed when a requested upstream ref cannot be resolved;
- add focused adversarial regression coverage and document the integrity/provenance boundary.

## Security boundaries

The checksum manifest detects inconsistency or corruption but does not authenticate the backup producer. Operators must still treat backups as sensitive, trusted recovery inputs. This PR also does not make mutable upstream branches or container tags immutable; provenance and immutable-input policy remain separate follow-up work.

## Validation

